          "type": "text",
          "text": "I added `\"eval\"` to `forbidden_builtins` in `checks.json`. The standard check (`python3 check.py`, per the README) now fails if any file under `src/` calls `eval`. I didn't need to change `check.py`: it already reads the code's structure instead of searching the text, and it only flags direct calls to a bare name.\n\nI tested it on a throwaway copy of the setup:\n- **This repo:** passes. `src/app.py` doesn't use `eval`.\n- **Should be allowed:** `eval(x)` in a comment, `\"eval(1)\"` in a string and `obj.eval(2)` all pass.\n- **Should be blocked:** a file calling `eval(\"1+1\")` fails the check, which names the file.\n\n**Limitation:** the check only catches `eval(...)` written out directly. It misses `builtins.eval(...)` because that has the same form as `obj.eval(...)`, which you asked to keep allowed. It also misses indirect uses, like `f = eval; f(x)` or `getattr(builtins, \"eval\")`. Catching those would need extra logic in `check.py`; I can add it if you want."
